ARM: OMAP2: power management back (pm24xx), for the N800/N810

Upstream removed OMAP2 power management; the sleep code, power and
clock domains and the PRCM accessors are still there. This is 3.1's
pm24xx.c for current kernels, with Nokia's 2.6.21 N800/N810 behaviour:

- idle: with no CORE functional clock running (a UART used as console
  keeps one), no DMA and sleep allowed, the whole chip goes to
  retention, the SDRAM in self-refresh (omap24xx_cpu_suspend from
  SRAM); otherwise the MPU powerdomain retains when no MMC, UART,
  McSPI or DSS clock runs, or just waits for an interrupt, from SRAM
  (omap24xx_idle_loop_suspend, back in sleep24xx.S); never during I2C.
- suspend to RAM: full retention without the GPT1 wake-up.
- PRCM: autoidle, CORE retention with memory retained, DSP and GFX off,
  hardware-supervised clock domains, automatic VCORE transitions (roof
  active, floor in retention), GPIO and GPT1 wake-ups.
- Maemo's /sys/power/sleep_while_idle (linuxrc sets it, BME clears it
  while charging) gates full-chip retention, as in Nokia's kernel.
- N8x0: the Menelaus platform data (hardware VCORE 1.40/1.05 V,
  regulators asleep in retention, as Nokia's board-n800-pm.c) reached no
  device, pdata-quirks' auxdata being for platform devices; run its late
  init on the bound I2C client.

--- a/arch/arm/mach-omap2/sleep24xx.S
+++ b/arch/arm/mach-omap2/sleep24xx.S
@@ -23,6 +23,22 @@
 	.text
 
 /*
+ * omap24xx_idle_loop_suspend() - Wait for interrupt, from SRAM (copied
+ * there at boot), for the MPU idle of pm24xx.c, also with the MPU
+ * powerdomain in retention. When the OMAP wakes up it continues execution
+ * at the point it went to sleep.
+ */
+	.align	3
+ENTRY(omap24xx_idle_loop_suspend)
+	stmfd	sp!, {r0, lr}		@ save registers on stack
+	mov	r0, #0			@ clear for mcr setup
+	mcr	p15, 0, r0, c7, c0, 4	@ wait for interrupt
+	ldmfd	sp!, {r0, pc}		@ restore regs and return
+
+ENTRY(omap24xx_idle_loop_suspend_sz)
+	.word	. - omap24xx_idle_loop_suspend
+
+/*
  * omap24xx_cpu_suspend() - Forces OMAP into deep sleep state by completing
  * SDRC shutdown then ARM shutdown.  Upon wake MPU is back on so just restore
  * SDRC.
